content.text:
Need also initial racePos default 3 -> 4 in markup, done. Good.

Now mobile showroom car/overlay. Let's adjust camera for narrow screens. In update:
```
const host = $('#showroom-canvas'); const narrow = host && host.clientWidth < 540;
const targetY = narrow ? 3.2 : ...
camera.position.set(narrow ? 1.7 : 2.1, targetY, this.dist*(narrow?1.12:.97));
camera.lookAt(0, narrow?0.45:.64, 0);
```
But `dist` default 8.4; mobile canvas 390x350. Zoom out by 12% and raise height. Car width ~3.9; with fov 34 at 8.4 distance, visible width at depth ~ 2*8.4*tan(17°)*aspect? Vertical visible height 5.1, horizontal 5.7. Car width 3.9 fits with margin. At mobile, aspect 1.11, horizontal 5.7 still. Car appears 300px wide because it's 3.9/5.7=68% of width ~265 px; screenshot car spans ~300 of 390. To leave overlay room below, need car moved up or shrink to ~240. Raise camera y (more top-down) compresses depth but car length projects shorter, not width. Move camera farther: dist 9.8 gives width 6.6, car 59% ~230. Good. And lookAt y .8 maybe. Let's set narrow dist factor 1.16 and fov 38? FOV 42 would make visible width 2*8.4*tan21*1.11=7.1, car 55%. Camera distance 8.4 and fov 42 might be enough. resize already sets fov 42 only if aspect<.9. Change condition to aspect < 1.2 or host width < 520. But camera fov 42 on mobile, plus no distance change, car ~220. Good. Set `this.camera.fov = aspect < .9 ? 46 : aspect < 1.2 ? 42 : ...`. At 390/350 aspect 1.11 => fov42. Car projects ~245. Better. Also lookAt y .7.

However the canvas on mobile may be 390x350; aspect 1.11. Use 42. Do that. Also maybe shift camera lookAt down slightly to move car up; leave .64. The car will still be near center vertically, overlay at bottom ~150px covers lower part. The car center at y=175, height ~120px extent 115-235, overlay starts at 200 (bottom 150px) so lower wheels under overlay. Could raise car by lowering lookAt to .2. That shifts car up maybe 20px. Let's set lookAt y=.45 on narrow. In update we need know narrow. But current `lookAt(0,.64,0)` fixed. Use:
```
const host = $('#showroom-canvas');
const narrow = host && host.clientWidth < 540;
this.camera.lookAt(0, narrow ? .38 : .64, 0);
```
This will make the car appear higher on screen (because camera pitch less downward? Actually looking lower tilts camera down, moving ground/car up). Yes.

Set fov 42 for aspect<1.2. Let's edit.

